This is a very common accounting requirement! In many core banking and ERP systems, **Credits are stored as positive values** and **Debits are stored as negative values** at the balance level.
Since your system currently treats Debits as positive (REQ_AMOUNT > 0) when inserting into the transaction tables, we just need to invert the sign mathematically before we merge it into the GL_BALANCE table.
You only need to change **one line** in your PROCESS_JOURNAL_BATCH procedure.
### The Fix
Open your PROCESS_JOURNAL_BATCH procedure and locate **Step 3. Merge GL Balances**.
You are going to change SUM(j.REQ_AMOUNT) as TXN_AMOUNT to SUM(j.REQ_AMOUNT * -1) as TXN_AMOUNT.
Here is the exact updated MERGE block to copy and paste over your old Step 3:
```sql
    -- 3. Merge GL Balances
    MERGE INTO GL_BALANCE target
    USING (
        SELECT j.REQ_BRANCH_CODE, j.REQ_CURRENCY, j.REQ_CGL,
               NVL(j.REQ_CSV_DATE, TRUNC(SYSDATE)) as BAL_DATE,
               -- FIX: Multiply by -1 so Debits (Positive REQ_AMOUNT) subtract from balance, 
               -- and Credits (Negative REQ_AMOUNT) add to balance.
               SUM(j.REQ_AMOUNT * -1) as TXN_AMOUNT,
               CASE WHEN j.REQ_CURRENCY = 'INR' THEN 1 ELSE MAX(c.CURRENCY_RATE) END as EXCH_RATE
        FROM JOURNAL_REQUEST j
        LEFT JOIN CURRENCY_MASTER c ON j.REQ_CURRENCY = c.CURRENCY_CODE AND c.FLAG = 1
        WHERE j.BATCH_ID = p_batch_id AND j.REQ_STATUS = 'P'
        GROUP BY j.REQ_BRANCH_CODE, j.REQ_CURRENCY, j.REQ_CGL, j.REQ_CSV_DATE
    ) source
    ON (target.BRANCH_CODE = source.REQ_BRANCH_CODE
        AND target.CURRENCY = source.REQ_CURRENCY
        AND target.CGL = source.REQ_CGL
        AND target.BALANCE_DATE = source.BAL_DATE)
    WHEN MATCHED THEN
        UPDATE SET
            target.BALANCE = target.BALANCE + source.TXN_AMOUNT,
            target.INR_BALANCE = NVL(target.INR_BALANCE, 0) + (source.TXN_AMOUNT * source.EXCH_RATE)
    WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN
        INSERT (ID, BALANCE_DATE, BRANCH_CODE, CURRENCY, CGL, BALANCE, INR_BALANCE)
        VALUES (GL_BALANCE_SEQ.nextval, source.BAL_DATE, source.REQ_BRANCH_CODE,
                source.REQ_CURRENCY, source.REQ_CGL, source.TXN_AMOUNT,
                (source.TXN_AMOUNT * source.EXCH_RATE));

```
### Why this works so safely:
By making the change inside the USING (SELECT ...) clause, the inverted TXN_AMOUNT automatically cascades down to both the UPDATE logic (for existing balances) and the INSERT logic (for brand new balances), as well as the INR_BALANCE calculations.
Because we only inverted it in Step 3, your GL_TRANSACTIONS and MASTER_JOURNAL audit tables will still correctly log Debits and Credits exactly as the user submitted them!
